WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.194 --> 00:00:02.160
[arrows swish]

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:06.520
Fire arrows, as a rule,
get way overused in cinema.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:08.450
These are a little more
interesting, though.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:09.530
Hi, I'm Toby Capwell.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:10.830
I'm curator of arms and armor

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.170
at The Wallace Collection
in Central London.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.464
Since I was a kid, I've been riding horses

00:00:15.464 --> 00:00:16.750
and fighting in armor.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.970
I've taken part in competitions,

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:21.380
armored combat, all over the world.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:22.480
Today, we're going to be looking

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.522
at the treatment of medieval
arms and armor in cinema.

00:00:30.151 --> 00:00:32.460
Éomer: What business does an elf, a man,

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.420
and a dwarf have in the Riddermark?

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:35.600
Speak quickly!

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.150
Toby: Yeah, this is a great
entrance of a character.

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:42.530
You know, Tolkien imagined
the Rohirrim as Anglo-Saxons

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:45.100
who fought like knights on horseback.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.830
Because the Anglo-Saxons didn't.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.600
They rode horses, but
they got off to fight.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.410
They didn't fight on their horses.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:53.230
The design of the helmet is derived

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:56.203
from early medieval examples.

00:00:56.203 --> 00:00:58.370
He really should have
his chin strap done up.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:02.030
The helmet is riding a
bit too high on his head.

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:03.467
The brow of the helmet,

00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:05.975
the horizontal edge there,

00:01:05.975 --> 00:01:07.875
should be right down on his eyebrows

00:01:07.875 --> 00:01:10.110
to really protect the front of the skull.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.690
You want to bring that
down as far as it can go,

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.120
and it can go right
down onto your eyebrows.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.060
So, I'll stick this on. All right.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.741
This is one of my own, made for me.

00:01:19.741 --> 00:01:22.650
[helmet clatters]

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:23.610
OK?

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:27.460
Now, you see, that brow
is right down on my eyes,

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:31.650
and it's sticking out
beyond my skull, right?

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:35.330
So a weapon comes in, it can't get to me.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:39.164
The skull, it might get
there, but that's still safe.

00:01:39.164 --> 00:01:41.320
Gimli: Give me your name, horse-master.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.400
Toby: You know, things were
often more highly decorated,

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.920
even for practical fighting,

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.740
than modern people often imagine.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:51.020
They're going for armor that
looks kind of architectural.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:53.880
Like, the dwarves build caves,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.010
and they work with rock,

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.130
and that's under mountains and stuff.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:01.330
So they're looking for a
kind of blocky aesthetic.

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:03.274
It's starting to look a bit too much

00:02:03.274 --> 00:02:06.300
like polyurethane here, to me.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.957
There's something about metal that --

00:02:08.957 --> 00:02:12.108
it has a weight, it has a rigidity

00:02:12.108 --> 00:02:15.050
that rubber and plastic and
polyurethane don't have.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.100
I gotta dock them a couple of points here

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.010
for the fit and the chin strap,

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:21.494
but it's still gotta be,
like, an eight out of 10.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:26.630
What's he doing?

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:29.000
I think his son was already
a knight at this point.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.970
I don't know why he feels
the need to knight him again.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:33.870
This is a total mishmash

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.990
of pretty good and pretty not so good.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:40.630
The mail that you're seeing
in close-up here is real mail,

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.540
and you can actually even see the rivets

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.890
that are closing each of the links.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.631
It's not fitting him well,

00:02:48.631 --> 00:02:50.281
and it's not tailored to him.

00:02:50.281 --> 00:02:51.973
It's something that's been just

00:02:51.973 --> 00:02:54.780
hoicked out of a van
and stuck on the actor.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.788
A lot of film mail

00:02:57.788 --> 00:02:59.921
is just, like, plastic links

00:02:59.921 --> 00:03:01.820
butted together, sprayed silver.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.453
For a lot of uses it's fine,

00:03:03.453 --> 00:03:04.411
but they don't have the rivets.

00:03:04.411 --> 00:03:07.910
Real mail is, you know,
iron links riveted closed.

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:10.460
I appreciate the fact that
he's got a lining under there.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.100
He's got padded textile under the mail,

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.950
which you have to have,
otherwise it is going

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:17.100
to do precisely nothing for you.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.400
A lot of exposed throats there.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.007
And they've stuck random
articulated shoulder plates

00:03:22.007 --> 00:03:23.490
over the mail shirt.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:26.700
This is Robert the Bruce
in the early 14th century.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:30.380
They were starting to
introduce more hard armor.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:33.270
They're a long way from full
plate armor at this period.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.010
They're a significant way away, really,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.180
from articulated constructions like that,

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:40.170
that look a bit like the tail
of a lobster or whatever.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:41.790
It's a later thing.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:46.160
And the shoulders were less
of a priority in this period.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:49.640
If you're gonna put plates
on a guy, in this period,

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.820
it should be on the elbows and the knees.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:55.852
And here you can see his
mail is a little off-center,

00:03:55.852 --> 00:03:58.026
is bagging around his throat.

00:03:58.026 --> 00:03:59.150
Just is bad.

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:00.512
The key thing about a mail hood

00:04:00.512 --> 00:04:02.820
is that it has a piece called a ventail,

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.330
which comes up and ties up to the chin.

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:08.350
And it's sculpted to
protect the whole throat.

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:10.670
You know, this is armor
that's doing nothing,

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:13.220
because I can stab him
straight through his windpipe.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:18.060
This film is actively
engaging with real heraldry.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:21.880
Heraldry is the system
of colors and shapes

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:26.110
put onto shields as a form
of visual identification.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:28.020
'Cause when all these guys are in armor,

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.795
it's hard to tell who's who.

00:04:29.795 --> 00:04:31.203
Heraldry was the answer.

00:04:31.203 --> 00:04:32.600
And it works very well.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:33.525
They got things wrong

00:04:33.525 --> 00:04:34.908
that they didn't have to get wrong,

00:04:34.908 --> 00:04:37.720
but they still did a
fair amount that's right.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:39.120
Seven out of 10.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:41.804
[arrow hits]
[both gasp]

00:04:43.055 --> 00:04:43.888
What?

00:04:43.888 --> 00:04:44.913
Nothing.

00:04:44.913 --> 00:04:47.793
Toby: I like this little scene because

00:04:47.793 --> 00:04:50.492
it shows that getting shot with arrows

00:04:50.492 --> 00:04:53.450
isn't immediately fatal.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:56.100
And Gilles de Rais here,
Joan's companion --

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:00.030
who was, incidentally, a
serial killer in real life,

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:04.598
he was a pretty despicable
child murderer --

00:05:04.598 --> 00:05:08.470
anyway, he knows that he can take a hit.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:11.460
She's not wearing armor
in this scene. He is.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.365
Shot for shot, a longbow arrow

00:05:14.365 --> 00:05:16.980
has very little chance
of hurting a man inside.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.020
You need the kind of mass volume

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.780
that Henry V organized in his armies

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:24.360
to put so many arrows downrange

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:27.280
that somebody's gonna
get hurt no matter what.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.220
Charles: I asked for a
grandiose coronation,

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:31.340
and this is what you give this?

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:33.550
Toby: He wouldn't do
that, almost certainly.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:35.620
He'd wear his coronation robes,

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.395
and all of his knights certainly wouldn't

00:05:37.395 --> 00:05:39.910
wear armor to a coronation.

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:43.840
Certainly not rusty,
banged-up stuff like that.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:46.440
Most of this is aluminum.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:48.420
This armor was made by Terry English,

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.832
who's one of the most
famous modern armorers

00:05:50.832 --> 00:05:52.140
working in cinema.

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:54.290
He's made armor for lots of things.

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:57.130
He made Arnold Schwarzenegger's
Mr. Freeze armor

00:05:57.130 --> 00:05:58.180
for the Batman movie,

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.010
he's made space marines
armor for "Aliens,"

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:03.210
and he's done a fair
amount of historical stuff.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:06.090
The king here is putting on gold armor.

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:07.170
It's gold colored.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:09.430
And this is actually good.

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:11.680
This doesn't look like gold armor.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:15.200
But fully gilded armor,
where you have steel armor,

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.090
and then it's copper-plated

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:20.635
and then it's fire-gilded,

00:06:20.635 --> 00:06:23.051
where you mix gold with mercury

00:06:23.051 --> 00:06:25.701
and you apply this mercury-gold mixture,

00:06:25.701 --> 00:06:28.900
if you then heat that mercury
up and you boil it off,

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:32.400
it leaves the gold chemically
bonded to the steel.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.600
Normally, most people wouldn't
be able to afford that.

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.232
I mean, really only kings can afford this.

00:06:37.232 --> 00:06:40.270
And not all kings, but the
French kings liked this.

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:43.220
It was a distinctive thing, actually.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:45.250
It creates an image of a king

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:48.870
as, like, the sun
manifested in human form,

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:50.530
like a superhuman being.

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:51.770
But it would have been nicer

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:55.160
if they'd given Terry the
budget to gild it properly.

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:57.720
I give it a four out of 10.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:00.575
Wolf: We've already been
through this before.

00:07:00.575 --> 00:07:02.550
We both know you haven't got it in you!

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:04.240
Toby: A sword is not a gun.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:06.730
Pointing a sword at someone out of range

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:08.350
with your arms fully extended

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:10.610
so you have nowhere to go with it

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:13.160
doesn't achieve anything
except making you look

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:16.300
like you've never held
a sword in your life.

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:18.320
You point your weapon out there like that,

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:19.753
he's gonna take it away from you.

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:25.310
There's often not enough color

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:27.960
in medieval depictions in cinema.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.010
Medieval people loved color,

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:31.450
bright color, richness.

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:35.025
It didn't populate their
world very much otherwise.

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:42.390
OK. So, you've got your leader,

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:45.280
your king, leading a cavalry charge.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:47.190
They're in wedge formation

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:49.289
with the king at the point of the wedge,

00:07:49.289 --> 00:07:50.320
which is great.

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:51.880
This is how it's done.

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:53.363
The guy on the point of the wedge

00:07:53.363 --> 00:07:55.570
is steering the whole cavalry charge.

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:58.460
He's like the guidance
system on a missile.

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:01.067
The wedge formation is one of the most

00:08:01.067 --> 00:08:04.267
effective formations for
a heavy cavalry charge.

00:08:04.267 --> 00:08:06.775
He's got his chin strap
done up. Love that.

00:08:06.775 --> 00:08:09.610
He's still got his
visor up, which is good,

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:11.184
because at this stage he's still

00:08:11.184 --> 00:08:13.383
far enough away from the enemy

00:08:13.383 --> 00:08:15.160
where he's got other
things to worry about.

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:18.520
A cavalry charge might
start when you're maybe

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:23.020
600 meters away from the enemy line,

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.940
but you're not going to
be full pelt straight off.

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:29.560
You need to start the
charge in good order.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:30.900
He's got to look around

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:34.150
and make sure his formation
is maintaining its position,

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:35.730
they're keeping their cohesion.

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:37.445
If he starts running too fast,

00:08:37.445 --> 00:08:39.044
then they have to catch up,

00:08:39.044 --> 00:08:40.994
and then they start
getting too spread out.

00:08:40.994 --> 00:08:42.180
They've got to stay close.

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:44.608
He's got to be watching that. Fine.

00:08:44.608 --> 00:08:46.870
But now the real test of the sequence,

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:48.560
and the thing that Hollywood hates to do,

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.670
is cover its famous actors' faces.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:53.370
But in a real cavalry charge, this guy,

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.461
once he's got himself set up,

00:08:55.461 --> 00:08:57.989
he needs to close that visor,

00:08:57.989 --> 00:08:59.510
and let's see if they do it.

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:01.210
He could close it right about now.

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:03.265
Now would be good.

00:09:04.245 --> 00:09:05.840
Oh, he's closed it!

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:07.622
I give it a seven.

00:09:07.622 --> 00:09:10.455
[swords clashing]

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:14.930
If I could change one
thing, I would change this.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:19.280
He's wearing his gorget on the
outside of his breastplate.

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:21.470
A gorget is that neck plate.

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:24.130
He should actually have
plates covering his throat.

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:25.612
The gorget has neck plates in here,

00:09:25.612 --> 00:09:28.050
and it comes down and has
this larger chest plate.

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:31.870
That chest plate should be
underneath the breastplate.

00:09:31.870 --> 00:09:34.180
The breastplate has a turned edge

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:36.780
to stop things sliding up into your face.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:39.920
But if anything does get
past, it hits the gorget.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:40.882
But in "Game of Thrones,"

00:09:40.882 --> 00:09:44.462
they habitually put them on the outside,

00:09:44.462 --> 00:09:48.388
and it's a howler that
I just, I can't abide.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:52.791
Fire!

00:09:55.311 --> 00:09:57.119
Toby: Fire arrows were a thing,

00:09:57.119 --> 00:09:58.670
especially in naval warfare,

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:01.745
when you've got key pieces of equipment

00:10:01.745 --> 00:10:02.770
you want to set on fire,

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:04.928
like sails and rigging and things.

00:10:04.928 --> 00:10:09.800
Fire arrows, as a rule,
get way overused in cinema.

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:11.440
These are a little more
interesting, though.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:13.020
There's something more
interesting going on.

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:14.287
You see, it's not just a lump

00:10:14.287 --> 00:10:16.670
of burning material stuck on an arrow.

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:20.223
They've actually got some
kind of explosive charge

00:10:20.223 --> 00:10:23.970
on these arrows that has a fuse.

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:28.370
And this is actually much
more historically plausible.

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:31.211
There are early incendiary manuals

00:10:31.211 --> 00:10:33.561
from the 15th and 16th centuries

00:10:33.561 --> 00:10:35.611
that give designs for different kinds

00:10:35.611 --> 00:10:39.130
of gunpowder bombs and
other explosive devices

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:43.081
to be put on arrows and crossbow bolts.

00:10:43.081 --> 00:10:44.378
[man groans]

00:10:44.378 --> 00:10:45.211
My Lord!

00:10:45.211 --> 00:10:46.770
[man groans]

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:48.460
Toby: I love this, right here.

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:51.560
That's armor doing its job, you know?

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:54.380
An arrow, when it hits you in full armor,

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:55.780
it'll probably glance off.

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:57.290
That's what armor's designed to do.

00:10:57.290 --> 00:11:00.560
But if it hits you square
and it gets enough purchase,

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.420
it can start to bite into the steel.

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:05.440
And if it hits you somewhere on the arm

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:08.850
or the shoulder where
the steel is thinner,

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:10.540
it might even get through.

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:14.208
Chances are most arrows are
not going to get through enough

00:11:14.208 --> 00:11:16.890
to do meaningful damage
to the person inside.

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:18.380
But they are going to get stuck,

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:20.980
and seeing a knight fighting on the walls

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:23.300
in a siege with arrows stuck in him

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:25.530
is the best thing I've seen all day.

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:27.200
10 out of 10, absolutely.

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:28.394
Just for that.

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:36.900
One of the absolute best
treatments of historical armor,

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:38.858
in any film ever,

00:11:38.858 --> 00:11:42.866
is in Laurence Olivier's
1944 film, "Henry V."

00:11:42.866 --> 00:11:44.760
But the film is set

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:46.268
and the design is led

00:11:46.268 --> 00:11:49.751
by the reality

00:11:49.751 --> 00:11:53.380
of clothing and equipment and architecture

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:55.760
and so forth of the early 15th century.

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:58.660
One of his historical
advisors was my predecessor

00:11:58.660 --> 00:11:59.793
at The Wallace Collection,

00:11:59.793 --> 00:12:03.190
the keeper of arms and armor
in the mid-20th century,

00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:04.523
Sir James Mann.

00:12:04.523 --> 00:12:06.720
And Olivier went to
The Wallace Collection,

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:09.474
and he saw the collection,
and he worked with James Mann,

00:12:09.474 --> 00:12:13.160
another arms and armor
scholar named Charles Beard,

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:16.067
and that film looks just like

00:12:16.067 --> 00:12:18.580
a 15th-century manuscript come to life.

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:22.223
And it's real steel armor
on all of these actors.

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:24.853
And it's one of the best ever.

